alkenes are useful synthetic  intermediates they're double bonds can  undergo a variety of chemical  transformations providing useful  functional groups like halides alcohols  and carbonyl compounds let's review some  of the major reactions that alkenes  undergo an alkene can be reacted with  hydrogen gas in the presence of a metal  catalyst to produce alkanes the hydrogen  gas adds to one face of the alkene so we  gets in addition of h2 across the double  bond alkenes react with  halogen-containing acids like  hydrochloric and hydrobromic acid adding  a single halogen across the double bond  if the alkene is reacted with acid in  the presence of water or an alcohol the  result is an alcohol or an ether water  or the alcohol can be used as solvent in  this reaction you may have noticed these  reactions are read yo selectively in our  example the new functional group is  added to the central carbon and not the  terminal carbon of the alkene this is  because these reactions form a carbo  cation intermediate the more substituted  carbocation forms because it is more  stable because carbo cations are  trigonal planar in geometry they can be  attacked by the nucleophile from the top  face or the bottom face so if there is a  stereocenter produced in the reaction it  will be a mixture of r and s when this  happens we say a racemic mixture is  produced the addition of the nucleophile  to the more substituted carbon of the  alkene is sometimes referred to as  markovnikov addition and is related to  carbo cation stability rearrangements  via hydride or alkyl shifts are possible  with these acid promoted reactions  treatment of an alkene with liquid  bromine or chlorine gas adds two atoms  of the halogen across the double bond  and if the reaction is carried out in  water a halo hydron is formed the latter  reaction is  again regioselective and stereoselective  a bromonium ion forms and the  nucleophile attacks at the more  substituted carbon atom because there is  significant contribution of the more  stable carbo cation resonance form here  this accounts for the regioselectivity  the functional groups are also added to  the molecule stereo selectively because  the bromonium ion blocks one face of the  molecule the nucleophile must approach  from the opposite side we call this anti  addition allylic bromination is a  radical reaction that adds a bromine  atom to the carbon adjacent to the  double bond while preserving the alkene  functional group oxy mercury a shin also  forms alcohols from alkenes in a  markovnikov fashion a mercury neum ion  is formed which reacts with water at the  more substituted carbon atom treatment  with sodium borohydride reduces the  mercury removing it from the molecule  the major advantage of this reaction is  that carbo cation rearrangements by  hydride or alkyl shift are not possible  although mercury is a rather toxic  element to use in the lab hydroboration  oxidation forms the less substituted  alcohol phien on markovnikov addition  boron is often substituted with large R  groups to make this reaction more  selective examples of commonly use  reagents are 9 BB n dice I a mole boring  and boring thf the large boron group  adds to the least sterically hindered  side of the alkene while the small  hydride atom is added to the more  substituted carbon atom treatment of the  boron complex with sodium hydroxide and  peroxide in a second stage of the  reaction produces the alcohol the  addition of two hydroxyl groups across  the alkyne is accomplished by treatment  with osmium tetroxide which forms an oz  mate ester this  action gives a sin dial because osmium  approaches from one face one molar  equivalent of a reducing agent is  required to cleave osmium from the  molecule and produce the dial ohsen  alisis essentially splits a double bond  in half creating a carbonyl compound  from each side in our example a  substituted aldehyde and formaldehyde  are formed we need to take a look at the  mechanism to understand what is going on  here the addition of ozone forms amylose  an ID which rearranges to form the ozen  ID in a second step dimethyl sulfate is  added this Cleaves the oxygen oxygen  bond and the charged intermediate  collapses to form carbonyl compounds and  dimethyl sulphoxide the vivek reaction  is an alkene forming reaction that seems  relevant to touch on here a carbonyl  compound is converted into an alkene by  treatment with an organophosphorus  reagent this vinegar II agent is a  phosphorus iLET although we can draw a  resonance structure with a double bond  there's also contribution from a neutral  dipolar form the carbon ion form makes  it a bit more apparent how the carbon  atom of the vit agree agent ends up  bonded to the electrophilic carbonyl  carbon of an aldehyde or ketone the side  product of this reaction is  triphenylphosphine oxide z alkenes are  favored under the classical vitug  conditions when there is a possibility  for resonance stabilization of the  carbon ion the e alkene is more favored  alkene metathesis though a bit more  advanced is an important reaction in  modern synthesis treatment of two  alkenes with molybdenum or ruthenium  catalysts result in the formation of a  new alkene with substitution from the  original alkenes on either side of the  double bond this reaction is most useful  when it is done in an intramolecular  fashion to close a ring
 

